{'type': 'chat_message', 'role': {'type': 'role', 'value': 'user'}, 'contents': [{'type': 'text', 'text': 'Validate the proposed solution for correctness, completeness, security,\ncommand consistency, and acceptance criteria. End with exactly DECISION: APPROVED\nor DECISION: REVISE, followed by actionable feedback.\n\nRequest:\nA2A remote Azure implementation - Design a production-ready private Azure Storage Account with lifecycle management\n\nPlan:\n### Plan: Production-Ready Private Azure Storage Account with Lifecycle Management\n\n#### High-Level Objective\nDesign and implement a production-ready private Azure Storage Account with lifecycle management. This solution must ensure secure access, compliance with organizational data retention policies, and optimized cost management via automated storage-tier transitions. The plan accounts for remote agent execution and validation using the A2A protocol path.\n\n---\n\n### 1. **Plan Breakdown (Numbered, Dependency-Aware)**\n\n---\n\n#### **Phase 1: Requirements Gathering and Initial Setup**\n1. **Define Business Requirements**  \n   - Collect retention policies, expected storage capacity, data usage patterns, access control requirements, and preferred region for deployment.  \n   - Document these details for alignment across all stakeholders (Planner, Executor, Validator).  \n   **Dependency:** None.  \n   **Risks:** Incomplete understanding of business requirements, leading to incorrect configuration.  \n   **Acceptance Criteria:** Clearly defined storage lifecycle and security requirements documented.\n\n2. **Azure Subscription and RBAC Verification**  \n   - Ensure Azure subscription details are shared with remote Executor and Validator agents.  \n   - Verify proper RBAC (Role-Based Access Control) permissions for both Executor and Validator agents.  \n   **Dependency:** Completion of Step 1.  \n   **Risks:** Incorrect RBAC settings, delays in granting permissions.  \n   **Acceptance Criteria:** Executor and Validator possess necessary permissions to provision and validate resources.\n\n---\n\n#### **Phase 2: Design and Deployment**\n3. **Storage Account Design**  \n   - Design a private Azure Storage Account using accessibility and security best practices:  \n     - Enable private endpoint connections for VNET integration.  \n     - Configure storage tiers (hot, cool, archive).  \n     - Activate soft deletes for blob recovery.  \n     - Define lifecycle management rules for automated data tiering and expiry.  \n   - Provide detailed design specs to Executor agents via templates or diagrams.  \n   **Dependency:** Completion of Steps 1–2.  \n   **Risks:** Design scope mismatch with requirements, incorrect tier configurations.  \n   **Acceptance Criteria:** Approved storage account design aligned with documented requirements.\n\n4. **Storage Account Deployment**  \n   - Execute deployment remotely using ARM templates, Terraform, or Bicep:  \n     - Create storage account in the preferred Azure region.  \n     - Configure lifecycle management policies to comply with organizational retention rules.  \n     - Establish VNET integration via private endpoint.  \n   - Confirm deployed storage account visibility and access.  \n   **Dependency:** Completion of Step 3.  \n   **Risks:** Deployment configuration errors, failure to connect private endpoints.  \n   **Acceptance Criteria:** Azure Storage Account is provisioned with lifecycle management configured properly.\n\n---\n\n#### **Phase 3: Security and Connectivity Configuration**\n5. **Access Control Setup**  \n   - Configure RBAC for storage account access (e.g., Storage Blob Data Contributor, Reader roles).  \n   - Establish Managed Identity-based access for workloads.  \n   - Restrict public access and validate private endpoint restrictions.  \n   **Dependency:** Completion of Step 4.  \n   **Risks:** Misconfiguration allowing unintended access, network security gaps.  \n   **Acceptance Criteria:** Storage account access is secure, private endpoint connectivity confirmed.\n\n6. **Data Encryption Configuration**  \n   - Verify storage account encryption using Azure-managed keys (default).  \n   - If necessary, integrate customer-managed keys (CMK) via Azure Key Vault.  \n   **Dependency:** Step 4 completion.  \n   **Risks:** CMK mismanagement leading to delayed encryption setup or data inaccessibility.  \n   **Acceptance Criteria:** Storage account encryption complies with organizational security standards.\n\n---\n\n#### **Phase 4: Testing and Validation**\n7. **Lifecycle Management Testing**  \n   - Upload a test dataset with metadata (tags, access times).  \n   - Validate lifecycle management policies by verifying automated movement of blobs between hot, cool, and archive tiers.  \n   **Dependency:** Completion of Steps 4–6.  \n   **Risks:** Errors in lifecycle rules triggering premature deletions or incorrect tier transitions.  \n   **Acceptance Criteria:** Lifecycle management rules function without errors and match retention policies.\n\n8. **Private Endpoint and Network Validation**  \n   - Confirm connectivity to the storage account only from the specified private endpoint.  \n   - Test access controls from all configured identities and roles to ensure least-privilege principles are applied.  \n   **Dependency:** Completion of Steps 4–6.  \n   **Risks:** Connectivity issues, unintentional public access.  \n   **Acceptance Criteria:** Access and network routing function only through private endpoints.\n\n9. **Validator Audit and Sign-Off**  \n   - Remote Validator agent reviews deployed architecture, lifecycle policies, and security configurations.  \n   - Executes organizational compliance checks for data privacy, encryption, and lifecycle policy.  \n   **Dependency:** Completion of Steps 1–8.  \n   **Risks:** Validator may require rework due to missed instructions or incomplete implementation.  \n   **Acceptance Criteria:** Validator confirms compliance with business requirements and Azure best practices.\n\n---\n\n#### **Phase 5: Handoff and Documentation**\n10. **Solution Handoff and Documentation Delivery**  \n    - Prepare and deliver detailed documentation, including:  \n      - Deployed resource details (e.g., account name, endpoints, lifecycle rules).  \n      - Diagrams showing architecture and private endpoint setup.  \n      - Security and access policies.  \n      - Lifecycle testing results.  \n    - Ensure stakeholders acknowledge receipt of documentation via formal validation.  \n    **Dependency:** Completion of Steps 1–9.  \n    **Risks:** Missing documentation causing future implementation errors.  \n    **Acceptance Criteria:** Handoff document is complete, detailed, and validated by stakeholders.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n### 2. **Risk Analysis**\n- **RBAC Permissions:** Incorrect setup might obstruct executor or validator access.  \n- **Deployment Issues:** Errors in storage account provisioning or lifecycle rules may delay go-live.  \n- **Connectivity Failures:** Incorrect private endpoint configurations could allow unwanted public access or block VNET communication.  \n- **Lifecycle Testing Failures:** Rules for tier transitions may not work as expected without adequate testing.  \n- **Documentation Gaps:** Unclear or incomplete documentation could result in subsequent mismanagement of the resources.  \n\n---\n\n### 3. **Acceptance Criteria**\n- **Private Storage Account:** Deployed with private endpoints and VNET integration.  \n- **Data Security:** RBAC permissions and encryption comply with organizational standards.  \n- **Lifecycle Management:** Automatic tier transitions and data retention rules work as expected.  \n- **Connectivity Validation:** No public access; private endpoints verified for secure communication.  \n- **Handoff Completion:** Comprehensive documentation delivered and acknowledged by stakeholders.  \n\n---\n\n### A2A Coordination Model Workflow:\n1. **Planner creates plan (Local)** ->  \n   `A2A_SendPlanToRemoteExecutor` ->  \n   2. **Executor provisions resources and configurations (Remote)** ->  \n   `A2A_SendSolutionToRemoteValidator` ->  \n   3. **Validator audits and confirms compliance (Remote)**.  \n\nThis structured and dependency-aware plan ensures a seamless deployment of a private Azure Storage Account with lifecycle management.\n\nProposed solution:\n### Solution: Production-Ready Private Azure Storage Account with Lifecycle Management  \nBelow is a full implementation using Azure CLI commands, ARM templates, lifecycle management testing, security configurations, and validation steps.  \n\n---\n\n## Phase 1: Requirements Gathering and Initial Setup  \n\n### 1. Define Business Requirements  \n**Implementation Steps:**  \n- Collect retention policy and expected storage capacity.  \n- Confirm preferred Azure region for deployment and access control requirements with stakeholders directly.  \n\n  Example regions for deployment:  \n  ```bash\n  az account list-locations --query "[].name" --output table\n  ```  \n\n**Verification Steps:**  \n- Send a documented list of requirements and confirmation with stakeholders (e.g., lifecycle policy: move blobs older than 30 days to cool tier, and blobs older than 365 days to archive tier).  \n- Verify clarity of requirements before proceeding.  \n\n---\n\n### 2. Azure Subscription and RBAC Verification  \n**Implementation Steps:**  \n- Check existing Azure subscriptions and validate RBAC assignments for remote agents:  \n  ```bash\n  az account show --query "{SubscriptionName:name, TenantId:tenantId}" --output table\n  az role assignment create --assignee <EXECUTOR_EMAIL> --role "Contributor" --scope /subscriptions/<SUBSCRIPTION_ID>\n  az role assignment create --assignee <VALIDATOR_EMAIL> --role "Reader" --scope /subscriptions/<SUBSCRIPTION_ID>\n  ```  \n\n**Verification Steps:**  \n- Confirm RBAC assignments:  \n  ```bash\n  az role assignment list --output table --query "[?principalName==\'<EXECUTOR_EMAIL>\']"\n  az role assignment list --output table --query "[?principalName==\'<VALIDATOR_EMAIL>\']"\n  ```  \n\n---\n\n## Phase 2: Design and Deployment  \n\n### 3. Storage Account Design  \n**Implementation Steps:**  \nDraft the architecture for a private Azure Storage Account:  \n- Activate private endpoints.  \n- Use storage tiers: hot, cool, and archive.  \n- Configure soft deletes for blobs and lifecycle rules.  \n\nExample ARM deployment template (`storageaccount.json`):  \n```json\n{\n  "$schema": "https://schema.management.azure.com/schemas/2019-04-01/deploymentTemplate.json#",\n  "contentVersion": "1.0.0.0",\n  "resources": [\n    {\n      "type": "Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts",\n      "apiVersion": "2022-09-01",\n      "name": "mystorageaccount",\n      "location": "eastus",\n      "properties": {\n        "accessTier": "Hot",\n        "allowBlobPublicAccess": false,\n        "minimumTlsVersion": "TLS1_2",\n        "networkAcls": {\n          "defaultAction": "Deny",\n          "virtualNetworkRules": [\n            {\n              "id": "/subscriptions/<SUBSCRIPTION_ID>/resourceGroups/<RESOURCE_GROUP>/providers/Microsoft.Network/virtualNetworks/MyVnet"\n            }\n          ]\n        },\n        "supportsHttpsTrafficOnly": true\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      "type": "Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts/blobServices/containers",\n      "apiVersion": "2022-09-01",\n      "name": "mystorageaccount/default/container1",\n      "properties": {\n        "publicAccess": "None"\n      }\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```  \n\n**Verification Steps:**  \nValidate the template:  \n```bash\naz deployment sub validate --template-file storageaccount.json --location eastus\n```  \n\n---\n\n### 4. Storage Account Deployment  \n**Implementation Steps:**  \nDeploy the Storage Account and configure lifecycle management rules:  \n```bash\naz deployment group create --resource-group <RESOURCE_GROUP> --template-file storageaccount.json\n```  \n\nAssign lifecycle policies via CLI or JSON template:  \nJSON template for lifecycle policies (`lifecycle.json`):  \n```json\n{\n  "rules": [\n    {\n      "enabled": true,\n      "name": "MoveToCoolTier",\n      "definition": {\n        "actions": {\n          "baseBlob": {\n            "tierToCool": {\n              "daysAfterModificationGreaterThan": 30\n            }\n          }\n        },\n        "filters": {\n          "blobTypes": ["blockBlob"]\n        }\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      "enabled": true,\n      "name": "MoveToArchiveTier",\n      "definition": {\n        "actions": {\n          "baseBlob": {\n            "tierToArchive": {\n              "daysAfterModificationGreaterThan": 365\n            }\n          }\n        },\n        "filters": {\n          "blobTypes": ["blockBlob"]\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```  \nApply lifecycle rules:  \n```bash\naz storage account blob-service-properties update --account-name <STORAGE_ACCOUNT_NAME> --resource-group <RESOURCE_GROUP> --set "{\\"policy\\": $(cat lifecycle.json)}"\n```  \n\n**Verification Steps:**  \nConfirm the lifecycle policy was applied:  \n```bash\naz storage account blob-service-properties show --account-name <STORAGE_ACCOUNT_NAME>\n```  \n\n---\n\n## Phase 3: Security and Connectivity Configuration  \n\n### 5. Access Control Setup  \n**Implementation Steps:**  \nRestrict public access and configure private endpoints:  \n```bash\naz storage account update --name <STORAGE_ACCOUNT_NAME> --resource-group <RESOURCE_GROUP> --allow-blob-public-access false\naz network private-endpoint create --resource-group <RESOURCE_GROUP> --name <PRIVATE_ENDPOINT_NAME> \\\n  --vnet-name <VNET_NAME> --subnet <SUBNET_NAME> --private-connection-resource-id /subscriptions/<SUBSCRIPTION_ID>/resourceGroups/<RESOURCE_GROUP>/providers/Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts/<STORAGE_ACCOUNT_NAME>\n```  \n\n**Verification Steps:**  \nValidate private endpoint connectivity:  \n```bash\naz storage account show --name <STORAGE_ACCOUNT_NAME> --resource-group <RESOURCE_GROUP> --query "networkAcls.virtualNetworkRules"\n```  \n\n---\n\n### 6. Data Encryption Configuration  \n**Implementation Steps:**  \nEnable encryption using Azure-managed keys or customer-managed keys (CMK):  \n```bash\naz storage account update --name <STORAGE_ACCOUNT_NAME> --resource-group <RESOURCE_GROUP> --encryption-key-type Microsoft.Keyvault\n```  \n\nIntegrate with Azure Key Vault:  \n```bash\naz keyvault key create --vault-name <KEYVAULT_NAME> --name <CMK_NAME> --protection software\naz storage account update --name <STORAGE_ACCOUNT_NAME> --resource-group <RESOURCE_GROUP> --key-name <CMK_NAME> --key-vault-uri <KEYVAULT_URI>\n```  \n\n**Verification Steps:**  \nConfirm encryption setup:  \n```bash\naz storage account show --name <STORAGE_ACCOUNT_NAME> --query "encryption"\n```  \n\n---\n\n## Phase 4: Testing and Validation  \n\n### 7. Lifecycle Management Testing  \n**Implementation Steps:**  \nUpload test blobs and validate automatic movement:  \n```bash\naz storage blob upload --container-name <CONTAINER_NAME> --file testfile.txt --account-name <STORAGE_ACCOUNT_NAME>\naz storage blob update --container-name <CONTAINER_NAME> --name testfile.txt --account-name <STORAGE_ACCOUNT_NAME> --tier Cool\n```  \n\n**Verification Steps:**  \nCheck tier transition:  \n```bash\naz storage blob show --container-name <CONTAINER_NAME> --name testfile.txt --account-name <STORAGE_ACCOUNT_NAME> --query "properties.accessTier"\n```  \n\n---\n\n### 8. Private Endpoint and Network Validation  \nConfirm private endpoint restrictions:  \n```bash\naz network private-link-resource list --name <PRIVATE_ENDPOINT_NAME> --resource-group <RESOURCE_GROUP>\n```  \n\nValidate connectivity using a controlled private environment:  \n```bash\ncurl -v https://<STORAGE_ACCOUNT_NAME>.blob.core.windows.net --resolve <STORAGE_ACCOUNT_NAME>.blob.core.windows.net:<PRIVATE_ENDPOINT_IP>\n```  \n\n---\n\n### 9. Validator Audit and Sign-Off  \nRemote Validator checks compliance via:  \n```bash\naz storage account show --name <STORAGE_ACCOUNT_NAME> --query "properties"\naz role assignment list --scope /subscriptions/<SUBSCRIPTION_ID> --resource-group <RESOURCE_GROUP>\naz security assessment list --query "[].displayName"\n```  \n\nValidator confirms alignment with policies and best practices.  \n\n---\n\n## Phase 5: Handoff and Documentation  \n\n### 10. Solution Handoff and Documentation Delivery  \nPackage documentation (storage design specs, lifecycle configurations, and access rules):  \n```bash\naz deployment export --name <DEPLOYMENT_NAME_FROM_HISTORY> --resource-group <RESOURCE_GROUP> --output export.json\n```  \n\nDeliver securely via Azure blob storage:  \n```bash\nazcopy copy "<LOCAL_DOCS_PATH>" "https://<STORAGE_ACCOUNT_NAME>.blob.core.windows.net/<CONTAINER>/<FILE_NAME>"\n```  \n\nPrompt client to verify delivery via acknowledgment.  \n\n---\n\n**Complete Implementation:**  \n- Private Azure Storage Account deployed and validated.  \n- Secure lifecycle management rules tested.  \n- Proper handoff documentation delivered.  \n\n'}], 'additional_properties': {}}
{'type': 'chat_message', 'role': {'type': 'role', 'value': 'user'}, 'contents': [{'type': 'text', 'text': 'Validate the proposed solution for correctness, completeness, security,\ncommand consistency, and acceptance criteria. End with exactly DECISION: APPROVED\nor DECISION: REVISE, followed by actionable feedback.\n\nRequest:\nA2A remote Azure implementation - Design a production-ready private Azure Storage Account with lifecycle management\n\nPlan:\n### Plan: Production-Ready Private Azure Storage Account with Lifecycle Management\n\n#### High-Level Objective\nDesign and implement a production-ready private Azure Storage Account with lifecycle management. This solution must ensure secure access, compliance with organizational data retention policies, and optimized cost management via automated storage-tier transitions. The plan accounts for remote agent execution and validation using the A2A protocol path.\n\n---\n\n### 1. **Plan Breakdown (Numbered, Dependency-Aware)**\n\n---\n\n#### **Phase 1: Requirements Gathering and Initial Setup**\n1. **Define Business Requirements**  \n   - Collect retention policies, expected storage capacity, data usage patterns, access control requirements, and preferred region for deployment.  \n   - Document these details for alignment across all stakeholders (Planner, Executor, Validator).  \n   **Dependency:** None.  \n   **Risks:** Incomplete understanding of business requirements, leading to incorrect configuration.  \n   **Acceptance Criteria:** Clearly defined storage lifecycle and security requirements documented.\n\n2. **Azure Subscription and RBAC Verification**  \n   - Ensure Azure subscription details are shared with remote Executor and Validator agents.  \n   - Verify proper RBAC (Role-Based Access Control) permissions for both Executor and Validator agents.  \n   **Dependency:** Completion of Step 1.  \n   **Risks:** Incorrect RBAC settings, delays in granting permissions.  \n   **Acceptance Criteria:** Executor and Validator possess necessary permissions to provision and validate resources.\n\n---\n\n#### **Phase 2: Design and Deployment**\n3. **Storage Account Design**  \n   - Design a private Azure Storage Account using accessibility and security best practices:  \n     - Enable private endpoint connections for VNET integration.  \n     - Configure storage tiers (hot, cool, archive).  \n     - Activate soft deletes for blob recovery.  \n     - Define lifecycle management rules for automated data tiering and expiry.  \n   - Provide detailed design specs to Executor agents via templates or diagrams.  \n   **Dependency:** Completion of Steps 1–2.  \n   **Risks:** Design scope mismatch with requirements, incorrect tier configurations.  \n   **Acceptance Criteria:** Approved storage account design aligned with documented requirements.\n\n4. **Storage Account Deployment**  \n   - Execute deployment remotely using ARM templates, Terraform, or Bicep:  \n     - Create storage account in the preferred Azure region.  \n     - Configure lifecycle management policies to comply with organizational retention rules.  \n     - Establish VNET integration via private endpoint.  \n   - Confirm deployed storage account visibility and access.  \n   **Dependency:** Completion of Step 3.  \n   **Risks:** Deployment configuration errors, failure to connect private endpoints.  \n   **Acceptance Criteria:** Azure Storage Account is provisioned with lifecycle management configured properly.\n\n---\n\n#### **Phase 3: Security and Connectivity Configuration**\n5. **Access Control Setup**  \n   - Configure RBAC for storage account access (e.g., Storage Blob Data Contributor, Reader roles).  \n   - Establish Managed Identity-based access for workloads.  \n   - Restrict public access and validate private endpoint restrictions.  \n   **Dependency:** Completion of Step 4.  \n   **Risks:** Misconfiguration allowing unintended access, network security gaps.  \n   **Acceptance Criteria:** Storage account access is secure, private endpoint connectivity confirmed.\n\n6. **Data Encryption Configuration**  \n   - Verify storage account encryption using Azure-managed keys (default).  \n   - If necessary, integrate customer-managed keys (CMK) via Azure Key Vault.  \n   **Dependency:** Step 4 completion.  \n   **Risks:** CMK mismanagement leading to delayed encryption setup or data inaccessibility.  \n   **Acceptance Criteria:** Storage account encryption complies with organizational security standards.\n\n---\n\n#### **Phase 4: Testing and Validation**\n7. **Lifecycle Management Testing**  \n   - Upload a test dataset with metadata (tags, access times).  \n   - Validate lifecycle management policies by verifying automated movement of blobs between hot, cool, and archive tiers.  \n   **Dependency:** Completion of Steps 4–6.  \n   **Risks:** Errors in lifecycle rules triggering premature deletions or incorrect tier transitions.  \n   **Acceptance Criteria:** Lifecycle management rules function without errors and match retention policies.\n\n8. **Private Endpoint and Network Validation**  \n   - Confirm connectivity to the storage account only from the specified private endpoint.  \n   - Test access controls from all configured identities and roles to ensure least-privilege principles are applied.  \n   **Dependency:** Completion of Steps 4–6.  \n   **Risks:** Connectivity issues, unintentional public access.  \n   **Acceptance Criteria:** Access and network routing function only through private endpoints.\n\n9. **Validator Audit and Sign-Off**  \n   - Remote Validator agent reviews deployed architecture, lifecycle policies, and security configurations.  \n   - Executes organizational compliance checks for data privacy, encryption, and lifecycle policy.  \n   **Dependency:** Completion of Steps 1–8.  \n   **Risks:** Validator may require rework due to missed instructions or incomplete implementation.  \n   **Acceptance Criteria:** Validator confirms compliance with business requirements and Azure best practices.\n\n---\n\n#### **Phase 5: Handoff and Documentation**\n10. **Solution Handoff and Documentation Delivery**  \n    - Prepare and deliver detailed documentation, including:  \n      - Deployed resource details (e.g., account name, endpoints, lifecycle rules).  \n      - Diagrams showing architecture and private endpoint setup.  \n      - Security and access policies.  \n      - Lifecycle testing results.  \n    - Ensure stakeholders acknowledge receipt of documentation via formal validation.  \n    **Dependency:** Completion of Steps 1–9.  \n    **Risks:** Missing documentation causing future implementation errors.  \n    **Acceptance Criteria:** Handoff document is complete, detailed, and validated by stakeholders.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n### 2. **Risk Analysis**\n- **RBAC Permissions:** Incorrect setup might obstruct executor or validator access.  \n- **Deployment Issues:** Errors in storage account provisioning or lifecycle rules may delay go-live.  \n- **Connectivity Failures:** Incorrect private endpoint configurations could allow unwanted public access or block VNET communication.  \n- **Lifecycle Testing Failures:** Rules for tier transitions may not work as expected without adequate testing.  \n- **Documentation Gaps:** Unclear or incomplete documentation could result in subsequent mismanagement of the resources.  \n\n---\n\n### 3. **Acceptance Criteria**\n- **Private Storage Account:** Deployed with private endpoints and VNET integration.  \n- **Data Security:** RBAC permissions and encryption comply with organizational standards.  \n- **Lifecycle Management:** Automatic tier transitions and data retention rules work as expected.  \n- **Connectivity Validation:** No public access; private endpoints verified for secure communication.  \n- **Handoff Completion:** Comprehensive documentation delivered and acknowledged by stakeholders.  \n\n---\n\n### A2A Coordination Model Workflow:\n1. **Planner creates plan (Local)** ->  \n   `A2A_SendPlanToRemoteExecutor` ->  \n   2. **Executor provisions resources and configurations (Remote)** ->  \n   `A2A_SendSolutionToRemoteValidator` ->  \n   3. **Validator audits and confirms compliance (Remote)**.  \n\nThis structured and dependency-aware plan ensures a seamless deployment of a private Azure Storage Account with lifecycle management.\n\nProposed solution:\n### Solution: Production-Ready Private Azure Storage Account with Lifecycle Management  \nBelow is a full implementation using Azure CLI commands, ARM templates, lifecycle management testing, security configurations, and validation steps.  \n\n---\n\n## Phase 1: Requirements Gathering and Initial Setup  \n\n### 1. Define Business Requirements  \n**Implementation Steps:**  \n- Collect retention policy and expected storage capacity.  \n- Confirm preferred Azure region for deployment and access control requirements with stakeholders directly.  \n\n  Example regions for deployment:  \n  ```bash\n  az account list-locations --query "[].name" --output table\n  ```  \n\n**Verification Steps:**  \n- Send a documented list of requirements and confirmation with stakeholders (e.g., lifecycle policy: move blobs older than 30 days to cool tier, and blobs older than 365 days to archive tier).  \n- Verify clarity of requirements before proceeding.  \n\n---\n\n### 2. Azure Subscription and RBAC Verification  \n**Implementation Steps:**  \n- Check existing Azure subscriptions and validate RBAC assignments for remote agents:  \n  ```bash\n  az account show --query "{SubscriptionName:name, TenantId:tenantId}" --output table\n  az role assignment create --assignee <EXECUTOR_EMAIL> --role "Contributor" --scope /subscriptions/<SUBSCRIPTION_ID>\n  az role assignment create --assignee <VALIDATOR_EMAIL> --role "Reader" --scope /subscriptions/<SUBSCRIPTION_ID>\n  ```  \n\n**Verification Steps:**  \n- Confirm RBAC assignments:  \n  ```bash\n  az role assignment list --output table --query "[?principalName==\'<EXECUTOR_EMAIL>\']"\n  az role assignment list --output table --query "[?principalName==\'<VALIDATOR_EMAIL>\']"\n  ```  \n\n---\n\n## Phase 2: Design and Deployment  \n\n### 3. Storage Account Design  \n**Implementation Steps:**  \nDraft the architecture for a private Azure Storage Account:  \n- Activate private endpoints.  \n- Use storage tiers: hot, cool, and archive.  \n- Configure soft deletes for blobs and lifecycle rules.  \n\nExample ARM deployment template (`storageaccount.json`):  \n```json\n{\n  "$schema": "https://schema.management.azure.com/schemas/2019-04-01/deploymentTemplate.json#",\n  "contentVersion": "1.0.0.0",\n  "resources": [\n    {\n      "type": "Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts",\n      "apiVersion": "2022-09-01",\n      "name": "mystorageaccount",\n      "location": "eastus",\n      "properties": {\n        "accessTier": "Hot",\n        "allowBlobPublicAccess": false,\n        "minimumTlsVersion": "TLS1_2",\n        "networkAcls": {\n          "defaultAction": "Deny",\n          "virtualNetworkRules": [\n            {\n              "id": "/subscriptions/<SUBSCRIPTION_ID>/resourceGroups/<RESOURCE_GROUP>/providers/Microsoft.Network/virtualNetworks/MyVnet"\n            }\n          ]\n        },\n        "supportsHttpsTrafficOnly": true\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      "type": "Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts/blobServices/containers",\n      "apiVersion": "2022-09-01",\n      "name": "mystorageaccount/default/container1",\n      "properties": {\n        "publicAccess": "None"\n      }\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```  \n\n**Verification Steps:**  \nValidate the template:  \n```bash\naz deployment sub validate --template-file storageaccount.json --location eastus\n```  \n\n---\n\n### 4. Storage Account Deployment  \n**Implementation Steps:**  \nDeploy the Storage Account and configure lifecycle management rules:  \n```bash\naz deployment group create --resource-group <RESOURCE_GROUP> --template-file storageaccount.json\n```  \n\nAssign lifecycle policies via CLI or JSON template:  \nJSON template for lifecycle policies (`lifecycle.json`):  \n```json\n{\n  "rules": [\n    {\n      "enabled": true,\n      "name": "MoveToCoolTier",\n      "definition": {\n        "actions": {\n          "baseBlob": {\n            "tierToCool": {\n              "daysAfterModificationGreaterThan": 30\n            }\n          }\n        },\n        "filters": {\n          "blobTypes": ["blockBlob"]\n        }\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      "enabled": true,\n      "name": "MoveToArchiveTier",\n      "definition": {\n        "actions": {\n          "baseBlob": {\n            "tierToArchive": {\n              "daysAfterModificationGreaterThan": 365\n            }\n          }\n        },\n        "filters": {\n          "blobTypes": ["blockBlob"]\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```  \nApply lifecycle rules:  \n```bash\naz storage account blob-service-properties update --account-name <STORAGE_ACCOUNT_NAME> --resource-group <RESOURCE_GROUP> --set "{\\"policy\\": $(cat lifecycle.json)}"\n```  \n\n**Verification Steps:**  \nConfirm the lifecycle policy was applied:  \n```bash\naz storage account blob-service-properties show --account-name <STORAGE_ACCOUNT_NAME>\n```  \n\n---\n\n## Phase 3: Security and Connectivity Configuration  \n\n### 5. Access Control Setup  \n**Implementation Steps:**  \nRestrict public access and configure private endpoints:  \n```bash\naz storage account update --name <STORAGE_ACCOUNT_NAME> --resource-group <RESOURCE_GROUP> --allow-blob-public-access false\naz network private-endpoint create --resource-group <RESOURCE_GROUP> --name <PRIVATE_ENDPOINT_NAME> \\\n  --vnet-name <VNET_NAME> --subnet <SUBNET_NAME> --private-connection-resource-id /subscriptions/<SUBSCRIPTION_ID>/resourceGroups/<RESOURCE_GROUP>/providers/Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts/<STORAGE_ACCOUNT_NAME>\n```  \n\n**Verification Steps:**  \nValidate private endpoint connectivity:  \n```bash\naz storage account show --name <STORAGE_ACCOUNT_NAME> --resource-group <RESOURCE_GROUP> --query "networkAcls.virtualNetworkRules"\n```  \n\n---\n\n### 6. Data Encryption Configuration  \n**Implementation Steps:**  \nEnable encryption using Azure-managed keys or customer-managed keys (CMK):  \n```bash\naz storage account update --name <STORAGE_ACCOUNT_NAME> --resource-group <RESOURCE_GROUP> --encryption-key-type Microsoft.Keyvault\n```  \n\nIntegrate with Azure Key Vault:  \n```bash\naz keyvault key create --vault-name <KEYVAULT_NAME> --name <CMK_NAME> --protection software\naz storage account update --name <STORAGE_ACCOUNT_NAME> --resource-group <RESOURCE_GROUP> --key-name <CMK_NAME> --key-vault-uri <KEYVAULT_URI>\n```  \n\n**Verification Steps:**  \nConfirm encryption setup:  \n```bash\naz storage account show --name <STORAGE_ACCOUNT_NAME> --query "encryption"\n```  \n\n---\n\n## Phase 4: Testing and Validation  \n\n### 7. Lifecycle Management Testing  \n**Implementation Steps:**  \nUpload test blobs and validate automatic movement:  \n```bash\naz storage blob upload --container-name <CONTAINER_NAME> --file testfile.txt --account-name <STORAGE_ACCOUNT_NAME>\naz storage blob update --container-name <CONTAINER_NAME> --name testfile.txt --account-name <STORAGE_ACCOUNT_NAME> --tier Cool\n```  \n\n**Verification Steps:**  \nCheck tier transition:  \n```bash\naz storage blob show --container-name <CONTAINER_NAME> --name testfile.txt --account-name <STORAGE_ACCOUNT_NAME> --query "properties.accessTier"\n```  \n\n---\n\n### 8. Private Endpoint and Network Validation  \nConfirm private endpoint restrictions:  \n```bash\naz network private-link-resource list --name <PRIVATE_ENDPOINT_NAME> --resource-group <RESOURCE_GROUP>\n```  \n\nValidate connectivity using a controlled private environment:  \n```bash\ncurl -v https://<STORAGE_ACCOUNT_NAME>.blob.core.windows.net --resolve <STORAGE_ACCOUNT_NAME>.blob.core.windows.net:<PRIVATE_ENDPOINT_IP>\n```  \n\n---\n\n### 9. Validator Audit and Sign-Off  \nRemote Validator checks compliance via:  \n```bash\naz storage account show --name <STORAGE_ACCOUNT_NAME> --query "properties"\naz role assignment list --scope /subscriptions/<SUBSCRIPTION_ID> --resource-group <RESOURCE_GROUP>\naz security assessment list --query "[].displayName"\n```  \n\nValidator confirms alignment with policies and best practices.  \n\n---\n\n## Phase 5: Handoff and Documentation  \n\n### 10. Solution Handoff and Documentation Delivery  \nPackage documentation (storage design specs, lifecycle configurations, and access rules):  \n```bash\naz deployment export --name <DEPLOYMENT_NAME_FROM_HISTORY> --resource-group <RESOURCE_GROUP> --output export.json\n```  \n\nDeliver securely via Azure blob storage:  \n```bash\nazcopy copy "<LOCAL_DOCS_PATH>" "https://<STORAGE_ACCOUNT_NAME>.blob.core.windows.net/<CONTAINER>/<FILE_NAME>"\n```  \n\nPrompt client to verify delivery via acknowledgment.  \n\n---\n\n**Complete Implementation:**  \n- Private Azure Storage Account deployed and validated.  \n- Secure lifecycle management rules tested.  \n- Proper handoff documentation delivered.  \n\n'}], 'additional_properties': {}}
